// TYPE_MODEL.md: "A bare `implements Copyable` *without* `(bare: …)` is a compile error." SPEC says the
// same thing at the `copy`/`give` rules, and `copyable_no_method` guards the neighbouring claim (declaring
// the contract without a public `copy` constructor) — but nothing covered the missing `(bare: …)` itself.
//
// The declaration is what a BARE hand-off means for this type: `Copyable(bare: give)` moves,
// `Copyable(bare: copy)` deep-copies. Defaulting it either way would make `R r2 = r1;` mean something the
// author never wrote, in the one place where guessing wrong is either a surprise deep copy or a surprise
// move — so kama makes the author say which, rather than picking. There is deliberately no
// "ambiguous — must annotate" error at the USE site; the annotation is required here, once.
type resource R implements Copyable<This> {
    int32 n;
    public ctor make(int32 n) { this.n = n; }
    public ctor copy(ref R source) { this.n = source.n; }
    ~R() {}
}

fn int32 main() {
    R a = R.make(n: 1);   // ERROR is on the `implements` above, not here
    return 0;
}
